digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and numerically evaluate experimentally feasible, unit-filled subwavelength atomic arrays in optical lattices as a high-transmission negative-refraction platform, testing whether cooperative non-local dipole interactions can excite transverse Bloch bands with in-plane group velocities opposite to incident quasimomentum and yield an effective negative index without conventional metamaterial resonators.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a paper establishes motivation, platform feasibility, simulation evidence, robustness, and physical design mechanisms for a target optical effect, these conclusions jointly support the paper-level aim to develop and evaluate that platform."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, metamaterials aim to overcome limitations of natural materials."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, simulations showing deflection opposite to normal behavior support interpreting the modeled system as having a negative effective refractive index."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, the atomic-array platform's high-transmission and robust negative refraction supports and motivates pursuing it as a research direction for practical negative refraction."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, engineering access to subradiant excitations is a method to increase the magnitude of the effective refractive index in the negative-refraction platform."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, negative refraction is associated with transformative optical applications such as cloaking and unconventional lensing/superlensing concepts."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, photonic-crystal negative refraction is limited by operating only over a narrow range of incidence angles."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, intrinsic optical losses in metamaterials and narrow incidence-angle operation in photonic-crystal negative refraction indicate practical limitations in existing optical negative-refraction platforms."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, the absence of a demonstrated practical application of negative refraction of light motivates the need for a new platform or mechanism."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, experiments support cooperative optical response in atomic arrays, including subradiant response, spectral narrowing below the single-atom decay limit, and spatial control of an atomically thin mirror."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, an approach using experimentally feasible subwavelength lattices and controlled cooperative interactions is more realistic than schemes requiring unattainably high refractive index."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, the simulations include exact recurrent-scattering treatment between stationary atoms in the low-light-intensity limit."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, exact recurrent-scattering simulations in the low-intensity limit together with realistic atomic transitions and Rb lattice spacing make the simulation evidence relevant to the proposed atomic-array platform."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, the evidence base combines motivation for practical negative-refraction platforms, feasibility of atomic-media routes, simulation support for negative effective index, high-transmission robustness, Bloch-band design guidance, and subradiant-mode index enhancement."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, feasible subwavelength atomic arrays with controlled cooperative response are a plausible setting to investigate negative refraction enabled by cooperative non-local atomic responses."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, experimentally feasible subwavelength unit-filled arrays can be used as a controllable geometry to harness cooperative non-local responses for engineered refraction."];
  "nI_bundle_case_support_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple established intermediate conclusions jointly support a summarized situation statement, then that summary can serve as the specific case input for final bundling."];
  "nI_choice" [label="(0,0,0) If an approach uses experimentally confirmed cooperative interactions and feasible subwavelength lattices, then it is more realistic than schemes requiring unattainably high refractive index."];
  "nI_contrast" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that prior atomic schemes require exceptionally high refractive index beyond experimental limits and the rule that such requirements imply infeasibility, therefore prior atomic schemes are experimentally unrealistic and a different mechanism is preferable."];
  "nI_contrast_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If an approach requires exceptionally high refractive index at high densities beyond experimental limits, then it is not experimentally feasible now."];
  "nI_def_to_n" [label="(0,0,0) If a transmitted/refracted beam deflects to the opposite side compared to positive refraction under the same geometry, then the effective refractive index inferred via Snell-like interpretation is negative."];
  "nI_design" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given that simulated refraction matches Bloch-band collective-resonance phenomenology and knowledge that antiparallel group velocity vs quasimomentum bands produce negative refraction, therefore research should focus on engineering those bands in subwavelength atomic arrays."];
  "nI_enh_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If coupling to subradiant excitations enhances the effective refractive index, then engineering access to subradiant modes is a method to increase |n| in the negative-refraction platform."];
  "nI_extract_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If simulations show opposite deflection and opposite deflection implies negative effective index, then one can extract a negative refractive index from those simulations."];
  "nI_gap_rule2" [label="(0,0,0) If a practical application of negative refraction of light has not yet been demonstrated, then there is a gap motivating the need for a new platform/mechanism."];
  "nI_limits_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple established approaches to optical negative refraction (metamaterial resonators; 3D photonic crystals) show loss/imperfection sensitivity/narrow operating ranges, then as a generalization existing platforms are limiting for practical use."];
  "nI_practical" [label="(0,0,0) If negative refraction is high-transmission and robust to imperfections over a useful parameter range, then it better supports practical optical applications than fragile/lossy alternatives."];
  "nI_practical_bridge" [label="(0,0,0) If a platform addresses the practicality gap for negative refraction of light, then it supports the need for a new platform and motivates pursuing that platform as a research direction."];
  "nI_practical_conc" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given demonstrated high transmission and robustness and the rule that these properties support practicality, therefore the atomic-array platform addresses the practicality gap."];
  "nI_s10_ref_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple experimental observations directly demonstrate cooperative response in atomic arrays, then as a generalization cooperative optical interactions in atomic arrays are confirmed."];
  "nI_s2_app_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a claim states a set of transformative applications, then named applications (e.g., cloaking, superlensing) are specific cases supporting that claim."];
  "nI_s3_v_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a statement about metamaterials includes an explicit development goal, then that goal statement is a supporting component of the overall metamaterials-development statement."];
  "nI_s5_v_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a limitation statement includes a specific operating restriction, then that restriction is a case supporting the overall limitation statement."];
  "nI_s7_conc" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the claim that atomic media can yield negative refraction without fabricated resonators and the viability rule, therefore atomic media is a viable candidate direction to address the need for a new platform."];
  "nI_s7_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If negative refraction can be obtained in atomic media without fabricated resonators, then an atomic-media route is a viable candidate direction when a new platform is needed."];
  "nI_sim_chain_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a work demonstrates negative refraction via detailed microscopic simulations and uses realistic transitions/lattice parameters, then the simulation evidence is relevant to the proposed platform claims."];
  "nI_sim_method_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a simulation exactly incorporates recurrent scattering and is exactly solvable in the low-intensity limit, then it provides strong methodological support for simulation-based demonstration claims."];
  "nI_sim_support" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given simulations showing opposite deflection and the rule that opposite deflection implies a negative effective index, therefore the system exhibits negative refraction (negative n) in the modeled conditions."];
  "nI_why_arrays_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If subwavelength unit-filled arrays are experimentally feasible, then they can be used as a controllable geometry to harness cooperative non-local responses for engineered refraction."];
  "nR22_10" [label="(10,22,10) Currently, experiments directly observe cooperative subradiant response of a 2D square atomic array in an optical lattice."];
  "nS12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, those earlier approaches require exceptionally high refractive indices at high density beyond current experimental capability."];
  "nS15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, simulations show a distinct deflection of propagating beams opposite to normal behavior."];
  "nS16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, the observed deflection lets one extract a negative refractive index from the simulations."];
  "nS17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, the work shows high-transmission negative refraction robust to missing atoms/position uncertainty across laser frequencies, incident angles, and lattice constants, using a stacked-layer momentum-space model."];
  "nS18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, large-scale simulations (including finite arrays) indicate the refraction matches transverse Bloch-band collective-resonance phenomenology."];
  "nS19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, negative refraction can be realized by engineering/exciting resonance bands whose in-plane group velocity is antiparallel to excitation quasimomentum (common in moderately subwavelength arrays)."];
  "nS20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, effective refractive index magnitude can be enhanced by coupling to more subradiant excitations."];
  "nS6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, a practical application of negative refraction of light has not yet been demonstrated."];
  "nS7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, it is claimed that negative refraction of light can be obtained in atomic media without artificially fabricated resonators."];
  "nS8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, cooperative, non-local atomic responses arising from strong light-mediated interactions at high density can enable negative refraction in atomic media."];
  "nS9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, controlled cooperative response is pursued via subwavelength atomic arrays with unit filling, feasible up to tens of wavelengths."];
  "nV13_2" [label="(13,2,0) Currently, the simulations exactly include recurrent scattering processes between stationary atoms in the low light intensity limit."];
  "nV2_1" [label="(2,1,0) Currently, negative refraction could enable cloaking applications."];
  "nV3_2" [label="(3,2,0) Currently, metamaterials aim to overcome limitations of natural materials."];
  "nV4_2" [label="(4,2,0) Currently, intrinsic non-radiative damping in metamaterials yields significant optical-frequency losses."];
  "nV5_3" [label="(5,3,0) Currently, photonic-crystal negative refraction operates only within a narrow range of incidence angles."];
  "nV9_2" [label="(9,2,0) Currently, subwavelength atomic arrays with unit filling can be experimentally realized at sizes up to tens of wavelengths."];
